-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Small Datasets/Sparse Inputs:** The method struggles when the transient mask lacks sufficient information to infer the inpainting process [S1].
-*   **Blurry Results:** Inpainting modules may produce blurry results and inconsistencies across views [S1]. Mitigated by using LPIPS loss inside the mask and depth supervision [S1].
-*   **High-Frequency Instability:** High-frequency signals cause instability in transient separation [S1]. Mitigated by starting training without positional encoding and incrementally boosting frequency (RegFre-IPE) [S1].
+**Pitfalls and Edge Cases:**
+*   **Small Datasets:** The method struggles with sparse inputs because the transient mask lacks sufficient information to guarantee accurate inpainting [S1].
+*   **Blurry Results:** Inpainting modules may produce blurry results or view inconsistencies. Mitigated by using LPIPS loss inside the mask and depth supervision [S1].
+*   **High-Frequency Instability:** High-frequency signals cause instability in transient separation. Mitigated by starting training without positional encoding and incrementally boosting frequency via RegFre-IPE [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on the Phototourism dataset (Brandenburg Gate, Sacre Coeur, Trevi Fountain, Taj Mahal) using 2x downsampled images [S1].
-    *   **Brandenburg Gate:** PSNR 25.33, SSIM 0.898, LPIPS 0.158 [S1]. Outperforms NeRF (18.90/0.816/0.232), NeRF-W (24.17/0.891/0.167), and Ha-NeRF (24.04/0.877/0.139) [S1].
-    *   **Ablation on Training Strategy (Brandenburg Gate):** RegFre-IPE (25.33/0.898/0.158) > IPE (23.58/0.864/0.191) > WT-IPE (19.86/0.723/0.289) [S1].
-    *   **Ablation on Mask Generation:** IE-NeRF(Ours) (25.33/0.898/0.158) > IE-NeRF(SM) (24.65/0.879/0.187) > IE-NeRF(IM) (22.27/0.802/0.191) [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Effective at separating static and transient components in uncontrolled environments without requiring 3D transient fields or semantic priors [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Struggles with small datasets and sparse inputs; may overlook shadows or images on walls if relying on semantic masks (though IE-NeRF(Ours) avoids this) [S1].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Uses mip-NeRF cone casting, which is more computationally expensive than single-ray sampling but handles multiscale representations better [S1]. The dual MLP strategy (coarse and fine) adds overhead [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the Phototourism dataset (Brandenburg Gate), IE-NeRF achieved PSNR 25.33, SSIM 0.898, and LPIPS 0.158, outperforming NeRF (18.90/0.816/0.232), NeRF-W (24.17/0.891/0.167), and Ha-NeRF (24.04/0.877/0.139) [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance degrades with sparse inputs or small datasets [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively separates static and transient components in uncontrolled environments without requiring 3D transient fields or external semantic segmentation models [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Struggles with small datasets; inpainting artifacts may persist if the mask is inaccurate; camera pose inaccuracies affect performance [S1].
 
 ## Background
-IE-NeRF was proposed by Wang et al. from Sun Yat-sen University and Pengcheng Laboratory [S1]. It was published in *Neurocomputing* in 2024 (arXiv:2407.10695v2, Dec 8, 2024) [S1]. The method builds on [Model Architecture Modifications](11313-model-architecture-modifications.md) [G]. It addresses limitations of NeRF-W and Ha-NeRF by using a single-stage inpainting-enhanced pipeline rather than dual NeRFs or semantic segmentation priors [S1].
+IE-NeRF was proposed by Wang et al. from Sun Yat-sen University and Pengcheng Laboratory, published in 2024 [S1]. It builds on the NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) and addresses the limitations of NeRF-W, which uses a 3D transient field, and prior-assisted methods like SF-NeRF, which rely on semantic segmentation [S1]. It is evaluated on the Phototourism dataset, including scenes like Brandenburg Gate, Sacre Coeur, Trevi Fountain, and Taj Mahal [S1].
 
